ine infusions for refractory pain. Which additional safety measure is most appropriate?", "choice_a": "Omit laboratory monitoring if the first infusion was tolerated", "choice_b": "Use serum ketamine concentrations as the only safety assessment", "choice_c": "Consider baseline and postinfusion liver function tests based on individual risk", "choice_d": "Check liver function only if the patient develops emergence agitation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The chronic-pain consensus recommends considering baseline and postinfusion liver function tests case by case in patients with baseline liver dysfunction, increased hepatic risk, or anticipated high-dose ketamine exposure at frequent intervals. This is a grade C recommendation with low-certainty evidence, not a requirement for identical testing after every brief perioperative dose (Cohen et al., 2018).", "difficulty": "medium", "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6023575/"], "question_number": 22, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q22"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which of the following is TRUE about the ASRA/AAPM/ASA acute pain consensus (Schwenk et al., 2018) as it applies to CRNAs?", "choice_a": "Ketamine is prohibited as a stand-alone acute-pain agent", "choice_b": "Ketamine may be used as a stand-alone acute-pain treatment or as an adjunct to opioids", "choice_c": "Intranasal ketamine is not recognized as a route", "choice_d": "PCA ketamine is contraindicated", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Schwenk et al. 2018 supports ketamine as stand-alone therapy, opioid adjunct, and (to a lesser extent) via the intranasal route, and addresses PCA use.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 23, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q23", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "A patient develops emergence agitation with visual hallucinations after ketamine sedation. The most evidence-supported intervention is:", "choice_a": "Immediate physostigmin